BY
Tyler O''Neill
Tampa
UPDATED 6:44 AM ET Jul. 23, 2025
PUBLISHED 6:10 PM ET Jul. 18, 2025
PUBLISHED 6:10 PM EDT Jul. 18, 2025
SHARE
HIGHLAND PARK Fla. — A Florida nonprofit is on a mission to revive the state’s farmland with sustainability in mind.
Biotech Applied Research
is turning waste into “biochar,” a charcoal-like material.
The nonprofit said it could completely change the Florida agriculture industry.
What You Need To Know
Biotech Applied Research (BAR)
said it has the first mobile biochar processing facility in Florida
Biochar is a charcoal-like material
BAR said biochar will benefit soil, water and air quality
